
SCC is an OpenShift security mechanism that controls what a Pod/container is allowed to do on a cluster.
A simple way to remember it:
SCC decides how securely a container is allowed to run.

For example, SCC can control whether a container:
- Can run as root
- Must run as a non-root user
- Can use privileged mode
- Can use host networking
- Can access host directories
- Can use Linux capabilities
- Can use specific SELinux contexts
- Can change its user/group IDs

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Why does OpenShift need SCC?
Imagine someone deploys this container:
containers:
- name: myapp
  image: myapp:1.0
  securityContext:
    privileged: true

A privileged container has much more access to the underlying host.
Without security restrictions, a compromised application could potentially have much greater access to the node.
OpenShift therefore puts security restrictions around Pods.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Example: Running as root
Suppose an image contains:

FROM ubuntu
USER root
CMD ["bash"]

The application wants to run as:
UID = 0
UID 0 means root in Linux.

OpenShift can use SCC to prevent that.
For example, an SCC may require:
MustRunAsNonRoot

Then:
Container requests root
       ↓
      SCC
       ↓
    DENIED

This is one of the major security concepts in OpenShift.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. What does an SCC control?
Let's look at the important fields.

A. runAsUser
Controls which Linux user ID the container can run as.
For example:

runAsUser:
  type: MustRunAs
  uid: 1000

This means the container must run as UID 1000.

B. runAsNonRoot
This prevents a container from running as root.
Conceptually:
UID 0
 ↓
Root
 ↓
Not allowed

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Linux capabilities
Linux divides certain root-like privileges into capabilities.
Examples include:
NET_ADMIN
NET_RAW
SYS_ADMIN
SYS_PTRACE

An SCC can control which capabilities a container is allowed to use.

For example:
Container
   │
   ├── NET_RAW
   ├── NET_ADMIN
   └── SYS_ADMIN

SCC can restrict these.
This is another layer of container security.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Host networking
Normally, a Pod has its own network namespace.
For example:
Node
│
├── Pod A
│    └── Pod network
│
└── Pod B
     └── Pod network

An SCC can control whether a Pod is allowed to use:
hostNetwork: true

If allowed:
Node network
     │
     └── Pod

This gives the Pod much closer access to the node's network environment.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. Host filesystem
A Pod might request access to the host filesystem.
For example:
volumes:
- name: host
  hostPath:
    path: /etc

This can be dangerous because /etc belongs to the host.
SCC can restrict host filesystem access.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

8. SELinux
OpenShift makes heavy use of SELinux for additional isolation.
SELinux can help ensure that a container is restricted from accessing resources it shouldn't access, even if normal Linux permissions might otherwise allow it.
Think of security as multiple layers:
              OpenShift
                  │
        ┌─────────┴─────────┐
        │                   │
       SCC                SELinux
        │                   │
        ▼                   ▼
 Container security     Resource isolation

You don't need to master SELinux immediately, but you should know that it is an important part of OpenShift security.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

9. Important SCC types
OpenShift commonly has several predefined SCCs.
You can see them with:
oc get scc

You may see entries such as:

NAME                  PRIV   CAPS   SELINUX    RUNASUSER
anyuid                false  ...    ...        RunAsAny
hostaccess            false  ...    ...        RunAsAny
hostmount-anyuid      false  ...    ...        RunAsAny
hostnetwork           false  ...    ...        RunAsAny
nonroot               false  ...    ...        MustRunAsNonRoot
nonroot-v2            false  ...    ...        MustRunAsNonRoot
privileged            true   ...    ...        RunAsAny
restricted-v2         false  ...    ...        MustRunAsRange

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

10. restricted-v2
This is an important SCC to understand.
restricted-v2 is designed to provide a relatively restrictive security configuration.
It generally prevents applications from simply choosing arbitrary privileged settings.

For example, an application shouldn't assume:
I can run as root.

Instead, OpenShift can assign an appropriate non-root UID from the project's allowed range.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

11. anyuid
Another important SCC is:
anyuid

It allows a container to run with any UID, including potentially UID 0.

Example

Application requests:

UID = 0

       ↓

     anyuid

       ↓

    Allowed


This can be useful for applications that genuinely require a particular UID, but it weakens some of the restrictions compared with a restrictive SCC.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

12. privileged
The:
privileged

SCC is much less restrictive.
It can allow things such as:
Privileged containers
Host access
Additional capabilities
Host networking
Host ports

depending on the SCC configuration.
You should not give applications the privileged SCC just to make an error disappear.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

13. How does SCC get assigned?
This is one of the most important parts.
Suppose you have:

User
 │
 ▼
ServiceAccount
 │
 ▼
Pod
 │
 ▼
SCC authorization

SCCs can be associated with users and groups, including service accounts.

For example:
ServiceAccount:
    myapp-sa

        │
        ▼

      SCC:
    nonroot

Then Pods running under that service account can potentially use that SCC, subject to OpenShift's SCC authorization and admission rules.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

14. ServiceAccount example
Create a service account:
oc create serviceaccount myapp-sa

Then your Deployment might specify:

spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: myapp-sa

      containers:
      - name: myapp
        image: myapp:1.0

The SCC authorization process determines which SCC the Pod can use.

15. Granting an SCC
For example, an administrator could grant an SCC to a service account:
oc adm policy add-scc-to-user anyuid \
  -z myapp-sa \
  -n myproject

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

17. How to see which SCC a Pod is using
This is very useful when troubleshooting.
You can inspect the Pod:
oc get pod <pod-name> -o yaml

Look for the SCC annotation, commonly:
openshift.io/scc: restricted-v2
